<DocsPage id="permissions">

          <p>
            Every tool action is checked against a ruleset whose effect is <strong>allow</strong>, <strong>ask</strong> or <strong>deny</strong>. Rules from the agent, from the project and from your saved answers are merged: deny wins, then ask, then allow, and anything with no matching rule defaults to ask. An agent with no permissions at all is denied everything rather than trusted.
          </p>
          <p>
            When you answer <strong>always</strong>, that allow is saved for the project, so the same action is not asked again. Saved answers are per project, and you can clear them.
          </p>
          <p class="note is-caution">
            For unattended runs the terminal agent takes <code>--auto</code> (also spelled <code>--yolo</code> or <code>--dangerously-skip-permissions</code>). It auto-approves anything not explicitly denied — deny rules still hold. It is off by default, and it is the flag to think twice about.
          </p>

          <h2 id="shell-sandbox">The shell sandbox</h2>
          <p>
            Commands an agent runs can be confined to their workspace by the operating system's own mechanism — a
            seatbelt profile on macOS, bubblewrap on Linux where it is installed — by setting
            <code>VECTOR_SHELL_SANDBOX</code> from 1.99.99. Releases before 1.99.99, such as desktop
            1.99.8 and npm CLI 1.99.7, do not read that name, so setting it there confines nothing.
          </p>
          <p class="note">
            It is opt-in and off by default, and Windows ships no equivalent, so on Windows Vector reports that it is
            running unconfined instead of implying protection it cannot provide.
          </p>
</DocsPage>
